    "reading": {
        "body": "Embedding models dominate actual AI usage far more than headline-grabbing large language models: the single most downloaded model in this snapshot accounts for 253 million downloads, more than five times the next entry, and four of the top five slots belong to sentence-transformer or embedding architectures.\n\nThe daily download series across the eighteen days covered runs between roughly 1.5 billion and 2.0 billion, with a visible step change upward around August 22. Before that date, daily figures cluster near 1.54 billion; after it, they cluster near 2.0 billion. The data does not explain what caused the shift, and it could reflect a pipeline change, a new automated consumer, or a large deployment event. What it does confirm is that at roughly two billion model-file retrievals per day across 16,369 tracked models, the infrastructure serving these downloads is substantial, even before accounting for the compute required to run the models once retrieved.\n\nThe licence breakdown covers the same 16,369 models and shows Apache 2.0 as the dominant terms by a wide margin, at 7,081 models, with MIT second at 2,300. Roughly 1,860 models carry no declared licence, and 1,684 fall under a catch-all \"other\" category. Together, undeclared and ambiguous licences cover more than 20 percent of the tracked catalogue, which matters for any operator trying to assess downstream legal exposure before committing infrastructure to a model. The data covers licence counts only, not download volume by licence type, so it does not show whether the openly licensed or the ambiguously licensed models are the ones actually being pulled at scale.",
